## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design streaming top-k algorithms within a sliding window using compact probabilistic data structures and articulate clear trade-offs between accuracy, space, and time.

## Key Concepts

- **Sliding window processing**: Maintaining state only over the most recent W elements
- **Space-Saving sketch**: A fixed-memory algorithm that provides approximate frequency counts
- **Top-k aggregation**: Identifying the k most frequent items in a stream
- **Approximation guarantees**: Bounding error in frequency estimates
- **Time/space complexity**: Analyzing per-operation and batch performance

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingTopK {
  constructor(k, W) {
    this.k = k;
    this.W = W;
    this.counters = new Map(); // item -> count
    this.window = []; // queue of last W items
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      const minEntry = this.findMinCounter();
      this.counters.delete(minEntry.key);
      this.counters.set(x, minEntry.value + 1);
    }
    this.window.push(x);
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      this.expire(this.window.shift());
    }
  }

  expire(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      const newCount = this.counters.get(x) - 1;
      if (newCount > 0) {
        this.counters.set(x, newCount);
      } else {
        this.counters.delete(x);
      }
    }
  }

  findMinCounter() {
    let minEntry = { key: null, value: Infinity };
    for (const [key, value] of this.counters) {
      if (value < minEntry.value) {
        minEntry = { key, value };
      }
    }
    return minEntry;
  }

  topK() {
    return [...this.counters.entries()]
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .slice(0, this.k);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a fixed-memory streaming algorithm for heavy hitter detection—a common requirement in real-world scenarios like analytics dashboards, network monitoring, and recommendation systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Misra-Gries algorithm**: A space-efficient algorithm for finding items that appear more than 1/m of the time
- **Heavy hitters**: Values that appear frequently in a stream (above a threshold)
- **Space-time tradeoffs**: Analyzing memory usage versus per-element update time

## Algorithm Analysis
| Metric | Complexity |
|--------|------------|
| Per-element update | O(1) |
| Per-batch query | O(m) = O(1) since m=2 |
| Memory usage | O(m) = O(2) = O(1) fixed |

## Code Example
```javascript
class MisraGries {
  constructor(m = 2) {
    this.m = m;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.m) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      for (const [key, val] of this.counters) {
        this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
        if (this.counters.get(key) === 0) this.counters.delete(key);
      }
    }
  }

  getHeavyHitters() {
    return Array.from(this.counters.entries())
      .filter(([_, count]) => count > 0)
      .map(([value, count]) => ({ value, count }));
  }
}
```

## Small Example (m=2, stream: 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2)
| Element | Counter State | Heavy Hitters |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| 1 | {1:1} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:1, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 1 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 3 | {1:1, 2:0} → {1:1} | 1 |
| 1 | {1:2} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |

## Follow-up Questions
- **Sliding windows**: Use a timestamped variant or combine with a separate structure tracking element ages
- **Ties**: Resolve by recency (most recently incremented) or arbitrarily since counts are equal
- **Accuracy**: Misra-Gries guarantees all heavy hitters are found but may include false positives; lowering the threshold (increasing m) reduces false positives at higher memory cost

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming heavy-hitters knowledge, practical analysis of tradeoffs, and ability to reason about accuracy vs memory.\n\n## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries heavy-hitters algorithm\n- Amortized per-item cost and fixed memory\n- Accuracy guarantees and when top-k is exact vs approximate\n\n## Code Example
```javascript
class MGHeavyHitters {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k; // top-k to track
    this.counters = new Map(); // id -> count
  }
  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k - 1) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      // decrement all counters
      for (let [id, cnt] of Array.from(this.counters)) {
        if (cnt === 1) this.counters.delete(id);
        else this.counters.set(id, cnt - 1);
      }
    }
  }
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a sliding window of size W?\n- How do you handle deletions or timestamped streams?\n- What are practical thresholds for choosing k in production?

